Fix Helix soft-wrap dropping double-width graphemes at the right edge.

Upstream: https://github.com/helix-editor/helix/issues/6585
A double-width grapheme (e.g. the CJK punctuation "，" or "。") that starts
in the last text column is never drawn: TextRenderer::column_in_bounds
requires `col + width <= viewport_width`, but DocumentFormatter only wraps
once the *start* column reaches the viewport width, so the grapheme is laid
out one column too far right and silently disappears. With an odd text width
or mixed-width text this happens often, which is why it shows up after
increasing the font size in the terminal.

Fix: wrap before the grapheme when `col + grapheme width` would exceed the
viewport. For a word that is already longer than `max_wrap` the buffered
text is left on the current line and the line break happens on the next
call, matching the existing long-word path; wrapping the whole buffer would
move the finished line down and still clip the grapheme.
Based on https://github.com/helix-editor/helix/pull/15712 (open).

--- a/helix-core/src/doc_formatter.rs
+++ b/helix-core/src/doc_formatter.rs
@@ -395,7 +395,25 @@
                     word_width = self.wrap_word();
                     col = self.visual_pos.col + word_width;
                 }
-                Ordering::Less => (),
+                Ordering::Less => {
+                    // A double-width grapheme that starts in the last column would
+                    // be skipped by the renderer (column_in_bounds requires
+                    // col + width <= viewport_width), hiding e.g. CJK punctuation
+                    // at the right edge. Wrap before that can happen.
+                    let viewport_width = self.text_fmt.viewport_width as usize;
+                    if self
+                        .peek_grapheme(col, char_pos)
+                        .is_some_and(|grapheme| col + grapheme.width() > viewport_width)
+                    {
+                        if word_width > self.text_fmt.max_wrap as usize {
+                            // Long word: fill the current line and break here.
+                            // Wrapping the whole buffer would move the finished
+                            // line down and still clip the last grapheme.
+                            return;
+                        }
+                        word_width = self.wrap_word();
+                    }
+                }
             }
 
             let Some(grapheme) = self.next_grapheme(col, char_pos) else {
